WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
[audience clapping]

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.570
- I'm not sure there's
anyone who's offered

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:03.730
better words of wisdom

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.969
about getting through
challenges and doubts,

00:00:05.969 --> 00:00:07.950
says my heart and soul;

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.830
and we have some people in the audience

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:11.010
who want her advice as well,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:11.909
So let's get to it.

00:00:11.909 --> 00:00:14.140
So where's Connor, and what
do you want to ask Connor?

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:14.973
- Hello.

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:16.679
Hi Glen, hi Kelly, nice to meet you guys.

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:17.620
- You look, James Dean.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.453
Okay.

00:00:18.453 --> 00:00:19.745
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:19.745 --> 00:00:22.454
- So like you, I recently
moved to Los Angeles.

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:25.455
I moved here in 2019 and
then the pandemic happened.

00:00:25.455 --> 00:00:28.955
I'm originally from Maine and
I went to college in Miami and

00:00:28.955 --> 00:00:30.789
I've always been super career focused.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:33.191
I work on the business side
of the entertainment industry.

00:00:33.191 --> 00:00:35.584
So I moved out here and was
like, here we go career.

00:00:35.584 --> 00:00:38.552
And I realized that once
a pandemic happened,

00:00:38.552 --> 00:00:41.153
my career really shouldn't
be my top priority.

00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:44.036
So I just turned 24. So I
feel like people are like, no,

00:00:44.036 --> 00:00:46.765
your twenties are when you,
you know, you really go for it.

00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:48.515
So I guess the question is,

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:51.036
should I continue to stick it
out here where I know there's

00:00:51.036 --> 00:00:53.111
more opportunity, or do
I go on the east coast,

00:00:53.111 --> 00:00:55.432
which is where my family and friends are,

00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:58.739
and I know that I'm probably
the happiest in the long run,

00:00:58.739 --> 00:00:59.572
if that makes sense.

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:00.447
- That's hard.

00:01:00.447 --> 00:01:01.280
- Sure does.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.113
- Yeah.

00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:03.665
- Connor, when you ask that question,

00:01:03.665 --> 00:01:04.498
I think about my sister,

00:01:04.498 --> 00:01:05.390
which is so strange, she's,

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.941
she's helping me host the
podcast and she's one of these,

00:01:07.941 --> 00:01:10.037
stick it out type people, never quit.

00:01:10.037 --> 00:01:12.089
Quitter. Quitting is for losers.

00:01:12.089 --> 00:01:15.750
And I'm more of like a right
now, live for right now.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.602
Quit everything, quit all the time.

00:01:17.602 --> 00:01:21.053
And so she would tell you
probably to stay for your career.

00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:22.245
And I would tell you probably to go,

00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:24.012
and we would both be wrong. Okay.

00:01:24.012 --> 00:01:27.551
Because the thing about when
people ask me what they should

00:01:27.551 --> 00:01:28.620
do, I don't know what to do,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.017
I don't know what to do,

00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:32.603
is that they always do know what to do.

00:01:32.603 --> 00:01:34.739
They just don't want to do the
thing that they know to do.

00:01:34.739 --> 00:01:36.877
Because the thing that
they know to do is really,

00:01:36.877 --> 00:01:38.726
really hard and uncomfortable
and going to rock the boat of

00:01:38.726 --> 00:01:40.823
their lives or someone else's life (beep).

00:01:40.823 --> 00:01:41.809
- That's real talk.

00:01:41.809 --> 00:01:42.967
- Right? So...

00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:46.550
- Damn! (blows raspberry)

00:01:48.114 --> 00:01:49.416
What?

00:01:49.416 --> 00:01:51.222
- So, the bad news Connor,

00:01:51.222 --> 00:01:53.616
is that I don't know what
you should do. Right.

00:01:53.616 --> 00:01:54.449
But the good news is that

00:01:54.449 --> 00:01:56.976
you definitely know what
you should do. Right?

00:01:56.976 --> 00:01:58.831
Because we just, it's so interesting.

00:01:58.831 --> 00:02:00.679
We're always asking
other people for advice,

00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:02.779
but the crazy thing about life
is that no one else has ever

00:02:02.779 --> 00:02:03.871
lived your life before.

00:02:03.871 --> 00:02:04.704
- That's true.

00:02:04.704 --> 00:02:05.537
- [Glennon] Right?

00:02:05.537 --> 00:02:06.951
It's an unprecedented,
unrepeatable experiment.

00:02:06.951 --> 00:02:07.784
- Yeah.

00:02:07.784 --> 00:02:08.617
- Right?

00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:10.339
So asking me what you should do,

00:02:10.339 --> 00:02:12.481
is like asking people for directions

00:02:12.481 --> 00:02:14.623
to places they've never been, right?

00:02:14.623 --> 00:02:16.597
There's no map that I can give you,

00:02:16.597 --> 00:02:19.579
but you do have an inner
compass, and all of life is just

00:02:19.579 --> 00:02:22.645
about honoring that inner
compass instead of other people's

00:02:22.645 --> 00:02:25.634
expectations of you. So do
that and keep doing that,

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:26.467
and you'll be fine.

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:27.300
(audience claps)

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.183
- I'll thank you down the road.

00:02:29.183 --> 00:02:30.197
- And also, I find it funny,

00:02:30.197 --> 00:02:31.877
because I used to think like that too.

00:02:31.877 --> 00:02:33.417
I'm like, oh, in my twenties,
I should be doing this,

00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:35.349
I should be doing this. Look, I'm like 39.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:36.861
I'm going to be 40, like next April.

00:02:36.861 --> 00:02:39.255
And I'm like, I still am
like, what makes me happy?

00:02:39.255 --> 00:02:40.263
Where should I go? What should I,

00:02:40.263 --> 00:02:42.405
you're always going to
have that conversation.

00:02:42.405 --> 00:02:44.652
Like just, you know,
ghost of Christmas future.

00:02:44.652 --> 00:02:46.836
It's like, it's never going to,

00:02:46.836 --> 00:02:48.600
you're gonna be 60 years
old probably going, man,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.448
I dunno, I could do this now.

00:02:50.448 --> 00:02:52.744
Like it's never, I wouldn't
say just cause your age.

00:02:52.744 --> 00:02:53.794
I just, I don't know.

00:02:53.794 --> 00:02:56.090
I always like to do, like,
what's going to make me happy.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.400
Like in the moment, like
what what's going to be fun.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.794
What seems exciting,
like kind of thing. But.

00:03:00.794 --> 00:03:02.264
- And you said that comment by the way.

00:03:02.264 --> 00:03:06.559
Yes. You said, 'Which will make
me happier in the long run?'

00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:08.001
Go with that one.

00:03:08.001 --> 00:03:08.834
- That's true.

00:03:08.834 --> 00:03:09.667
- Right? Go with that one.

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:12.743
- The latter. Okay, thank
you so much, Connor,

00:03:12.743 --> 00:03:16.410
and welcome to LA and
Godspeed if you leave.

00:03:17.716 --> 00:03:20.414
We Can Do Hard Things with
Glennon Doyle is available

00:03:20.414 --> 00:03:24.030
wherever you get your podcasts,
and also be sure to pick up

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.056
Untamed.

00:03:25.056 --> 00:03:27.274
I highly recommend it. It's so amazing.

00:03:27.274 --> 00:03:28.710
It's life-changing, in fact,

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.054
everyone in my audience is getting a copy.

00:03:31.054 --> 00:03:33.081
(audience cheers)

